    "teacherReflection": "Did learners discover ASF = k^2 from their own grid-paper counts, or did they rely on being told? Which learners still believed the cost should be KES 60 after the Explain phase, and what evidence changed (or did not change) their mind? Was the counting of H (1.5 x 2 cm) manageable on the grid, or should half-squares be taught beforehand? How well did learners distinguish LSF from ASF in their words and tables? Which new DQB questions will best lead into Lesson 7 on volume scale factor? What should I adjust in group roles or time so that every learner counts and explains at least once?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We drew prints S (3 x 4 cm), P (6 x 8 cm), L (9 x 12 cm) and H (1.5 x 2 cm) on grid paper and found areas of 12, 48, 108 and 3 cm2. Four copies of S fit exactly on P, and nine copies fit on L. The prices at KES 2.50 per cm2 were KES 30, 120, 270 and 7.50. The distorted 6 x 4 cm photo had area 24 cm2.",
      "learned": "When a figure is enlarged with linear scale factor k, its area is multiplied by the area scale factor ASF = k^2. For k = 2, ASF = 4; for k = 3, ASF = 9; for k = 1/2, ASF = 1/4. Cost follows area, not length. The rule needs both dimensions to be multiplied by the same k (similar figures).",
      "explained": "Achieng's print P has every length doubled (LSF 2), so its area is 2^2 = 4 times that of S (12 cm2 to 48 cm2). Mr Otieno charges KES 2.50 per cm2, so the price rises from KES 30 to KES 120, which is four times and not double. The distorted photo has only the width doubled, so its area (24 cm2) is only twice and it is not similar."
    }
